1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
T1 moves 40 from item x to item y: x goes 100 to 60, y goes 50 to 90.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The crash lands between the two writes: x has lost 40, y never received it.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Atomicity says both writes survive or neither does. The log is what makes that possible.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Recovery reads the log and rebuilds the state that the committed transactions alone define.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Every change is written twice: a log record first, then the data page.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Write-ahead logging: the log record reaches stable storage before the page it describes.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
A committed page may still be in memory at the crash. That is why redo exists.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
An uncommitted page may have been flushed early. That is why undo exists.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Five record types carry everything recovery needs.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
A write record keeps both values: old 100, new 60. Undo needs old, redo needs new.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
The checkpoint record names the transactions still running at that instant.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
An LSN orders the replay; without it, undo has no notion of newest first.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
One log, two transactions, a crash at the end, and no checkpoint.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
A start record puts its transaction in the undo list.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
A commit record moves the transaction from the undo list to the redo list.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The result: undo T1, redo T2. The two lists never share a transaction.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
The disk at the crash is not the truth: x holds 60, written by a transaction that never committed.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Redo walks forward: apply the new value of every write in the redo list.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Undo walks backward: apply the old value of every write in the undo list.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The result equals the initial state with only the committed transaction applied.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Two buffer decisions decide which of undo and redo you need.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Force or no-force: are pages flushed at commit? No-force means a committed change may be missing, so redo.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Steal or no-steal: may an uncommitted page be flushed? Steal means a dirty value may be on disk, so undo.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Deferred modification is the no-steal corner: only redo. Immediate modification is steal: both.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
One transaction writes x twice, 0 to 5 and 5 to 9, and never commits.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Undo in forward order leaves 5, a value that no committed transaction ever wrote.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Undo in reverse order leaves 0, the value before the transaction began.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Undo newest first, redo oldest first. Reversing either gives a value nothing wrote.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A checkpoint forces every dirty page and records which transactions are still running.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
T4 committed before the checkpoint, so its page is already on disk and T4 needs no redo.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
T1 started before the checkpoint and never committed: the active list puts it back in undo.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Reading back only to the checkpoint: undo T1 and T3, redo T2. Five records instead of eleven.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
What if the machine crashes again while it is recovering?

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
The log has not changed, so the same two lists are rebuilt and the same records replayed.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Redo and undo are idempotent: applying a record twice leaves the value it sets.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
GATE CSE 2021 Set 1 asked this, and the official key is the same undo and redo list.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Five steps: last checkpoint, its active list, scan to the crash, split the lists, replay.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Then check your answer: recovered state equals the committed-only state, and the lists are disjoint.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Boundary: a fuzzy checkpoint does not force pages, so you must read back further and redo more.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
One line: the log holds both values, so recovery can always rebuild the committed state.

